  "response": "When a laboratory report and a connoisseur's eye appear to point in opposite directions, the dispute is adjudicated not by ranking the two kinds of expertise but by specifying what each is competent to decide. The first step is to interrogate each physical technique for the limits built into its own procedure. Dendrochronology dates oak panel supports by matching tree-ring sequences to regional reference chronologies, and the structure of that matching procedure thereby determines exactly what such a date delivers. Dendrochronology yields an earliest possible felling date for the tree used in a panel. The technique establishes the exact year in which a panel painting was executed.\n\nConsider the terms in which a panel dispute is ordinarily posed. Either the oak panel came from a tree felled before 1550, or it came from a tree felled after 1550; one of these holds, and they cannot both hold. Whichever obtains narrows the field of candidate workshops without by itself naming a hand. Pigment identification supplies a second order of chronological marker. Titanium white became commercially available to artists in the 1920s. Titanium white appears routinely in paintings produced during the 1850s.\n\nWith pigments, stratigraphy matters as much as chemistry, since paint can be added to a surface long after the picture itself is finished. At least one of two accounts must hold, and perhaps both: the anachronistic pigment belongs to a later restoration campaign, or the painting was executed after the master's death. In the case at hand, the attribution committee concluded that the anachronistic pigment belonged to a nineteenth-century restoration rather than the original paint layer; in other words, the offending material came from a later campaign of repair and not from the artist's own hand, a finding that rules out any claim that the painting was executed after the master's death.\n\nVan Meegeren's forged paintings contained phenol-formaldehyde resin, and that chemical finding shows that Han van Meegeren was convicted of forgery in 1947. That such a resin could be detected at all indicates the general principle on which adjudication rests: technical evidence can exclude an attribution without establishing authorship. Connoisseurial judgment, by contrast, rests on comparison with securely documented works by the artist. The two forms of expertise therefore enter the argument at different points, and a responsible committee treats a laboratory result as a constraint on the field of possible authors rather than as a verdict about hands, reserving the positive ascription for the comparative argument while declining any ascription that the materials render impossible.",
